  "query_preview": "..\n(Monday night through Saturday)\nIssued at 1205 AM CST Sun Nov 15 2026\n\nWhile models generally show some reduction in cloud cover heading \nthrough the week, clearing may be slow given the cold surface \nairmass and continued persistence of low level moisture. Daytime \ntemperatures will be closely dependent on the amount of cloud cover, \nbut there is broad consensus that there should be a warmup of \nafternoon highs into at least the mid 60s for most by Wednesday. \nLows in this timeframe should continue to hold in the mid 40s to low \n50s.\n\nRidging over the area will keep the weather steady during the early \npart of the workweek, but the upper air pattern over the western US \nreverts back to more troughing over the course of the week. This \nbrings a return of southerly winds over our region, producing milder \nnights and warmer days as the cold and damp airmass is pushed away \nand replaced by a warmer Gulf one. A few showers are possible in \nthis pattern, but a lack of a stronger disturbance nearby and split \nflow at 250mb keeps rain chances on the lower side Thursday and \nFriday. Another amplified trough is depicted in the medium range \nguidance over the Intermountain West at the end of the week. This \ncould provide a better established opportunity for rain if it drops \nfarther south, but for the time being there is not much consistency \nin the signal.",
